---
import ToolPageLayout from "../../layouts/ToolPageLayout.astro";
import PriceListBuilderIsland from "../../components/calculator/PriceListBuilderIsland.tsx";

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Should every landscaper charge the same prices?",
    answer:
      "No. Your true cost of materials, labor, and overhead is specific to your business and your market. This tool lets you build your own price list rather than copying a generic rate sheet.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do I know if a price on my list is actually profitable?",
    answer:
      "You'd need your true cost per service to check that — this free tool just organizes the prices you charge. Landscape Estimate Pro's Service Rate Health compares each rate against your real cost and target margin automatically.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I print this price list for customers?",
    answer: "Yes — use the print button to produce a clean, printable price sheet.",
  },
];
---

<ToolPageLayout
  title="Landscaping Price List — Free Price Book Builder"
  description="Build and print a landscaping price list with your own service rates, units, and minimum project charge. Organize mulch, planting, edging, and other prices."
  eyebrow="Free interactive tool"
  h1="Landscaping Price List"
  intro="Build a per-service price book (also called a landscape price list) for your business — service, rate, and unit — plus your minimum project price. Print it for customers or keep it as your internal reference."
  breadcrumbLabel="Landscaping Price List"
  currentHref="/landscaping-price-list/"
  faqs={faqs}
  webApplicationSchema={false}
>
  <PriceListBuilderIsland client:load />

  <Fragment slot="extra">
    <article class="tool-editorial mb-10">
      <h2>Use a consistent unit for every service rate</h2>
      <p>A price book is useful only when the unit and scope are clear. “Mulch installation per cubic yard” differs from a material-only price per bag. A planting rate per shrub should identify what size and preparation it covers. Record these distinctions in the service description rather than expecting the unit price to explain them.</p>
      <h2>Build a rate from costs before publishing it</h2>
      <p>Use the <a href="/mulch-cost-calculator/">mulch cost calculator</a> and <a href="/topsoil-cost-calculator/">topsoil cost calculator</a> to work through material and installation assumptions. The <a href="/landscape-labor-cost-calculator/">labor calculator</a> helps price paid crew time. These tools use your inputs; the starting rates in this price list are examples, not market averages.</p>
      <p>Once you know true cost per service unit, check your proposed selling rate in the <a href="/landscape-profit-margin-calculator/">margin calculator</a>. A $60 cost sold at $100 earns $40 profit and 40% margin. Selling the same service for $80 earns $20 and 25% margin. A tidy-looking rate is not necessarily a profitable one.</p>
      <h2>Review the minimum project price separately</h2>
      <p>A small job can still need travel, loading, delivery, and cleanup. Set the minimum charge using those costs instead of multiplying only the smallest material quantity by a service rate. Review saved prices when supplier quotes or labor costs change, and confirm site conditions before presenting the list as a project quote.</p>
      <p>This free page organizes and prints prices; it does not calculate their profitability automatically. <a href="/landscaping-estimating-software/#service-rate-health">Pro's Rate Health</a> compares saved rates against service costs and your target margin.</p>
    </article>
    <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-paper p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink">See how Service Rate Health actually works</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">
        Read the walkthrough on the <a href="/landscaping-estimating-software/#service-rate-health" class="font-semibold text-forest underline">Landscape Estimate Pro</a> sales page —
        a worked example of a service flagged as underpriced and the rate required to fix it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPageLayout>
